WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.970
- Hello, YouTubiverse.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.980
Neil DeGrasse Tyson here,
your personal astrophysicist.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.890
I just got off of a video call

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.010
with the International Space Station,

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.810
a live conversation with
the two astronauts there.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:12.643
And who were they?

00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:15.170
We had Jessica Meir and Christina Koch.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.010
They've spacewalked together recently

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.810
and got a little bit of
that overview effect.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.150
So, in this brief call
we talked about food,

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:28.150
politics, Mars, and also,
I had to end with a comment

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.970
about what it's like to clean up vomit

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.460
in case that happens.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.457
Check it out.
(upbeat music)

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.290
- [Mission Control]
Station, this is Houston.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.510
Are you ready for the event?

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.600
- Houston, this is Station.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.976
We're ready for the event.

00:00:45.976 --> 00:00:46.809
- [Mission Control] "StarTalk",

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:48.360
this is Mission Control Houston,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.400
please call Station for a voice check.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.560
- Station, this is Neil DeGrasse Tyson

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.330
with "StarTalk Radio".

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:55.240
How do you hear me?

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.870
- Hello, Neil, we have you loud and clear.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.410
How do you hear us?

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.700
- Excellent, excellent.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.890
Let's do this.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.923
It's great to have this time with you.

00:01:02.923 --> 00:01:04.760
I think they only gave us 20 minutes,

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.830
that must be when you're in
this part of the hemisphere,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.780
so I'm gonna try to squeeze
in a whole lotta questions.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.260
Both of you, of course,
are scientifically trained,

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.390
and I'm just wondering,
what kind of science

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.230
and engineering tasks or
projects, or experiments,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.000
have you been conducting
while you've been up there

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.860
on the International Space Station.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.390
- Well, we have really been involved

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.020
in every type of science.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.020
So far, I have been up
here for about two months.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.020
I have been involved in an experiment

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.530
growing Mizuna lettuce,
that's actually located

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.390
right here next to us.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.050
You can see the two different
light wavelengths here,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.240
we've got a red and a blue wavelength,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.870
evaluating light quality and fertilizer.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.290
I've been involved in a variety

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.430
of physiological and medical studies.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.200
We have done experiments looking
at protein crystal growth,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.440
evaluating having potential
for future pharmaceuticals

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.340
for various drugs for disease states.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.170
We have been involved
in ultrasound studies,

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.900
looking at different physiological
aspects like blood flow,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.610
and how that changes in micro-gravity.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.950
Really, any type of science, you name it.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.910
Combustion experiments, for example,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.340
we have a combustion rack,

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.090
even flames, of course, burn
differently in micro-gravity.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.660
So, a little bit of a
sampling of everything,

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.240
and I think that's what keeps
our jobs so interesting,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.830
is that it's not just,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.030
for example, my particular
field of expertise,

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.270
it's really all the aspects of science.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.129
- Combustion experiments.

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:30.379
(laughing)

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.083
That doesn't sound safe.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.390
I assume there are fire
extinguishers all around Station.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.500
- That is true, there
are fire extinguishers

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.050
all around Station, but we do hope

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.290
to never have to use them on
our Combustion Integrated Rack,

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.520
the Scientific Payload Rack

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.540
where we do combustion experiments.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.780
They're in a chamber that, obviously,

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.870
has all the safety requirements needed.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.930
The interesting thing about
that type of experiment

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.040
is how differently flames
burn in micro-gravity

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.980
because there's no convection
to bring the hot air up

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.010
and create the shape of
flame that we're used to,

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.440
for example, on a candle.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.920
It's a very different environment,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.060
and it allows us to get to some

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.040
of the fundamental properties
of flames, and turns out,

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.860
it's a lot more complicated
than you might think,

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.430
and the results of those experiments

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.860
not only affect and benefit life on Earth

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.580
in terms of fuel efficiency and whatnot,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.210
but it also informs
things like flammability

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.500
for products for use in
space and space travel.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.220
- One of my favorite experiments
is when you burn a candle

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.390
in zero-G, and then it just exhausts

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.210
all the oxygen in its environment.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.410
And you're right, like you said,

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.640
without the convection, a
candle will self-extinguish,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.240
I guess you see this when
you do these experiments.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.420
- Yup, that's absolutely true,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.520
and we see other really
interesting evidence

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.300
of lack of convection here.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.270
Like for example, when
we take our food packets

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.630
out of our little oven
and set them on the table

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.080
to cool off, it takes a lot longer

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.040
unless we actually put them in an airpath.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.510
(laughing)

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:55.960
- So much we take for
granted here on Earth

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.700
just by living in one-G.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.250
I have a quick question.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.233
I gotta settle this once and for all.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.380
Every bit of physics I know

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.000
tells me you are in
free fall towards Earth,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.860
which renders all of you weightless,

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.480
and everything in the
International Space Station,

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.780
so I would call that zero-G.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.060
Where does NASA come
off calling it micro-G?

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.390
- Yep, that's a great question.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.130
I think the idea is just the concept

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.680
that everything that has mass

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.640
also produces a gravitational field,

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.950
so we might be wrong to call it zero-G

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.240
just because, obviously,
everything around me

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.980
does have a non zero gravitational force

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.370
associated with it.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.420
- Okay, but they're very
tiny gravitational forces,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.750
so we could just as easily
call it nano-G, couldn't we?

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.300
- Nano, pico, you know.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.960
I'm not sure they
actually calculated that,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.370
but it's definitely true
that we are all in free fall

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.830
around the planet, and we are experiencing

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.390
the gravitational pull of Earth,

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.520
which is actually what keeps
us in orbit around Earth.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.520
So, you could also look at it that way,

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.420
that we're not zero-G in that sense,

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.400
but the free fall aspect of zero-gravity,

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.780
I'd say we're in it.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.521
Did we land ourselves on the
right side of your argument?

00:05:05.521 --> 00:05:07.700
(laughing)

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.440
- I'm gonna look up all my metric prefixes

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.700
and see which one actually applies

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.500
to how much gravity each one
of you have on each other,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.608
and I'll get back to you on that.

00:05:16.608 --> 00:05:18.090
- Yeah, and I think the other difference

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.760
is that we are constantly disturbing

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.180
this micro-gravity environment.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.300
So, any time we input a motion,

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.990
if we're exercising on the treadmill,

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.886
or the cycle ergometer on ARED,

00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:31.940
we're disturbing that
pure zero-gravity force

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.470
that maybe an experiment desires

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:34.690
just by our human presence.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.950
So, we're still disturbing
that all the time.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:37.783
- So, let's talk about food.

00:05:37.783 --> 00:05:39.250
There're different segments

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.660
of the International Space Station

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.100
representing different
countries who participate.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.100
And so, do each of you
trade food with each other

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.842
when you get bored with
your meatloaf, or something?

00:05:51.842 --> 00:05:54.460
(laughing) I'm just curious
how much food exchange goes on

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.210
on the International Space Station.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.170
- So, NASA has a food
lab, which you may know,

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:01.180
and they develop most of a majority

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.720
of the food that we, as
American astronauts, eat.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.780
But the Russians also have a food lab,

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.850
and we are actually allowed to choose

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.210
a certain number of our food allocation

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.670
from the Russian food system.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:15.060
So, both Christina and I like
exotic and different foods,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.710
and so we went with a maximum
majority of Russian food

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:18.640
that we could add.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.300
We also have some foods developed
by European Space Agency

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.880
that we can add to that mix,

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.160
and we have a European Space
Agency astronaut up here,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.010
so we get to sample a
little bit more of that

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:30.620
because he has a little bit
more of that than other people.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.420
My goals was actually to fly
with a Japanese astronaut

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:34.960
because I love Japanese food,

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.060
and I really wanted some of that.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.080
But unfortunately, we
don't have any of that

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:39.410
up here right now.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:40.810
But we do tend to share,

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.360
the Russians will actually
bring other things down to us,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.420
and we'll trade favorites back and forth.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.530
So, I think variety is very
important for us up here,

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:50.900
even more important
than people would think.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.090
People that don't even care so much

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:53.910
about food on the ground do tend to,

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.710
in a long duration mission,
feel the need to have

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.400
some more variety and make a difference

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.350
in terms of just that boredom
that develops otherwise.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.540
- I've eaten in restaurants
in both Italy and in France,

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.870
and I'm guessing the French
and Italians have no interest

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.250
in trading food with
anybody else. (laughing)

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.010
- Well, our Italian is pretty generous.

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.870
I think one of the
highlights on the food system

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.320
is the lettuce experiment
that we talked about earlier.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.650
Part of that, instead of
sending all of the samples

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.370
down to be analyzed by the ground team,

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.520
we actually get to harvest
and eat the lettuce.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.240
So, we've had two harvests now

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.010
where we get this fresh
supplement lettuce to our diet,

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.180
and that's a real treat.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.410
And we'll have another harvest

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:31.940
the day before we celebrate Thanksgiving,

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.280
so we're looking forward to that.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.990
- So, I've been curious about that.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:39.180
For longterm space travel,

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.650
obviously there'll be some expectation

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.453
that you grow your own food.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.970
How close are we to growing
all of your own food

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.450
without requiring supply chains

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.570
either to and from the Space Station,

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:56.070
or to and from Mars, if that's
our ultimate destination.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.630
- Absolutely, great question.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.210
I think that right now we're at a place

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.030
where we're learning the best ways

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.490
that we can grow things in space,

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.140
but where we're really looking to go

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.580
is to develop systems that are one,

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:09.380
closed loop systems, so a
system where we're using

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:11.170
waste products from various sources

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:15.650
and feeding those in to the
actual plant development.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.100
And that can include our
carbon dioxide cycle,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.960
our oxygen cycle, and our water cycle,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.530
and we actually have some
really neat life support systems

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.540
onboard right now that are testing

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.390
some of those technologies.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:27.890
Another aspect of the food system

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:28.740
that really has to grow

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.270
would be incorporating proteins.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.940
Right now we have lettuce as
our main crop that we've grown,

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.430
and we've grown different things up there,

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.260
they've grown flowers, we're looking into

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.310
starting into vegetables and fruits,

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.080
but incorporating protein
sources like peanuts,

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.970
or maybe legumes, I
think, is a next big leap.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:46.630
And we're not quite there yet,

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.220
as Jessica mentioned, we've had salad,

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:52.010
and we've joked sometimes
that the caloric intake

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.140
that we've used so far in growing it

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:57.350
might be a little bit more
than what we end up consuming.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.910
But obviously the techniques
are what's important,

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.358
and proving out the systems

00:09:01.358 --> 00:09:03.920
and the operational concept.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:05.360
- I'm guessing that you're not gonna,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:09.010
any time soon, start
traveling with farm animals.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:11.530
So, likely all of your protein will come

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.160
from plant-based sources,

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:15.400
which would mean the
future space exploration

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.210
would be vegetarian, that's
what that sounds like.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.650
- Maybe, we're not sure.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.560
We also do have some other devices

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.520
that are pretty exciting
up here right now.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.100
We have one facility called
the BioFabrication Facility,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:31.090
which is actually, the
goal of that is to grow

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.610
human organs in space.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.630
Now, that's of course not
for food or consumption,

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:37.360
that is to contribute to the deficit

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:41.620
of organ for transplant
operations on the ground.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.280
By any kinds of efforts on the ground,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.970
when you are trying to
produce these tissues

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.360
and make these bioartificial tissues,

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:50.250
there are some problems

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.560
with the support structure for these.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.970
And so, the theory is, in micro-gravity,

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.290
we won't have that problem
with the support structure,

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.250
so we can better grow these
bioartificial tissues,

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.340
and then turn them into complete organs.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.550
Going a little bit along
with that with 3D printing,

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.060
we have other 3D printers up here

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:06.830
to make other pieces of hardware.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.960
The Russians also recently
tested some animal cells,

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.250
so, some animal protein cells

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:14.100
to see whether or not
they could 3D print meat.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:16.240
So, there are some
experiments that are trending

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.730
in that direction as well.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.880
- I don't know why I didn't
think of that, of course.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.080
If you can lab grow
proteins, meat proteins,

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.970
then you never needed the
animal in the first place,

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.170
and then there's another
source of protein for you.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:28.300
So, excellent.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.140
I'm just curious, can you imagine

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.230
a totally self-sustaining place on Mars?

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.390
How soon do you think we can have

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.950
a self-sustaining Mars colony?

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.410
Are we counting that in
decades now, not just years?

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.770
Because we know you can get water on Mars,

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.860
and fuel, maybe, rocket fuel.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.937
If you could just elaborate a little bit

00:10:50.937 --> 00:10:53.500
about what's there on Mars already

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:54.800
versus what, at least initially,

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.624
we've gotta bring there
to help you survive.

00:10:57.624 --> 00:10:58.457
- Sure.

00:10:58.457 --> 00:10:59.340
Looking at the current outlook

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.020
in some of NASA's longterm goals,

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.890
talking about going back
to the moon in the 2020s,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:07.910
and then back to the Mars
in maybe the late 2030s,

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.960
so that would be sort
of, an initial visit,

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.980
some of the baseline design concepts

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.080
would be for a 15-month
mission, a three-year mission,

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.880
somewhere in that range.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:18.580
To do a sustainable mission,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.540
I think we are definitely talking decades,

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:21.520
but I think it's important

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.160
to have the longterm goal always in mind.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:26.530
I've never been someone
that has been a fan

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.050
of terra transforming a planet

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.290
to make it more Earth-like.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:35.690
I think that the excitement
of going to a different planet

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.680
would be utilizing the in
situ resources that are there.

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.390
So, I would see something

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:43.530
like a sustainable Mars establishment,

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:47.940
to me, would always require
some type of resupply,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.990
and even if that's just to
make it livable and habitable

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.890
in terms of what humans think
of as habitable and livable,

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:56.800
I think is the important thing.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:59.220
But using the in situ resources as well.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:01.360
- Yeah, I think the...

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:05.600
If you were to drive
across the United States,

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.190
there's basically a supply
chain there already for you.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.410
You can stop at the
Quik Mart and get food,

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:11.990
and something already
brought the food there

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.270
for you to then eat before you arrived.

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.340
So, maybe the idea of supply chains

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.640
shouldn't be viewed as
something so foreign,

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.080
because we actually thrive on that here.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.510
I'm curious about the future of robotics,

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.060
and artificial intelligence.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.040
There's a whole camp, I'm not in this camp

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.310
that I'm about to describe,

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.173
that would say "Do all space
exploration with robots.

00:12:36.167 --> 00:12:37.977
"Do all space exploration with robots,

00:12:37.977 --> 00:12:39.527
"and then you don't even need the people,

00:12:39.527 --> 00:12:41.407
"you don't have to bring the robot back,

00:12:41.407 --> 00:12:42.770
"so they're cheaper."

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.070
And so, I know you guys are astronauts,

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:47.680
but I'm just wondering
what kind of thinking

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.680
you put into the idea of robot
astronauts for the future.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.190
- Yeah, I've heard a lot of
people debate that as well.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.717
Maybe "Why do we even care
about human space flight,

00:12:58.717 --> 00:13:00.570
"maybe we can do it all with robotics,"

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:02.340
and I think the answer
isn't one or the other,

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.340
it's really a blend.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.690
And the two can be complementary
for different purposes

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:08.220
and for different reasons.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.980
We, of course, have had a lot of success

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:13.523
sending satellites, and sending
rovers and other spacecraft.

00:13:14.462 --> 00:13:16.630
There have been several rovers on Mars

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.950
that have provided a
wealth of data already,

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:20.590
and we haven't been there yet as people,

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:23.040
so that's showing us that
there are some things

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:24.210
that we've already been able to do

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:25.530
with these robotic spacecraft

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.440
that we haven't done yet with humans.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.620
But of course, if you
look at the other side,

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:30.720
you can look at the statistics

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.360
and what these rovers
were able to accomplish

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.300
in terms of their exploration,
sending the data back,

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.850
then being controlled
by people on the ground

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.150
and having that relayed back

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.420
if they found something off-nominal,

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.830
or something where they wanted
to go a different direction,

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:46.820
that actually ends up taking months.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:48.660
And the statistic that I've seen,

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.590
is that what a rover can
do in months or even years,

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:54.830
astronauts can do in minutes or hours

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:56.370
just because we have the capability

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.740
to problem solve directly real-time,

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.580
and then redirect those necessary.

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:02.810
So, I would see the
future as involving both.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:05.270
I would love to go on a
mission with robots as well.

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:07.540
I mean, we could bring
humans and robots together.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:10.120
- So, just to be clear,
your phrase off-nominal

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.993
really to the rest of us just means weird.

00:14:12.993 --> 00:14:14.550
(laughing)

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:15.383
- That's right.

00:14:15.383 --> 00:14:16.710
Sorry for the vernacular there.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:18.263
Anything unexpected.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:21.680
What wasn't programmed in, for example,

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:24.680
but the people on the ground
as to what the course would be.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.980
If we saw something more interesting,

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.350
or more scientifically
valuable in another direction

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:31.770
we could redirect ourselves,

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.700
whereas the rover might be
programmed in one direction

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:36.640
and would miss out on an
opportunity like that.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.260
- Promise me, if any of you go to Mars

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:40.090
and you see something off-nominal

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:43.490
that you'll just say to NASA,
"I see something weird," okay?

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:46.660
I want you to promise me
here and now. (laughing)

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:48.792
- I'm not sure we can make
those kinda promises to you.

00:14:48.792 --> 00:14:50.650
(laughing)

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:53.793
- A quick question just about politics.

00:14:55.125 --> 00:14:56.110
You guys have spacewalked,

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:59.230
as so many visitors to the Station do,

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:02.830
you spacewalk together, and you see Earth.

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:04.470
And that Earth does not have

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.080
color-coded boundaries of countries.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.360
And so, surely you get the newsfeed

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:10.970
in who's in conflict with whom,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.700
and who's spying, and who's fighting.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.463
This overview effect, which
so much is written about,

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:23.560
how do you see the future
of politics in space

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:28.210
versus what's going on down here on Earth?

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.610
Can space be something that leads

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.550
all peace movements on Earth?

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.710
How much thinking have you guys done about

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:37.960
the role that space can
play in the future of this?

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.720
- We both have been fortunate enough

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.330
to experience that overview effect,

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:44.060
and I do think that it has the option

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:47.390
of improving the world in
the way that you talk about.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.670
One thing that we've learned is that,

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:50.690
we get the question a lot,

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:52.720
do politics on Earth inform the way

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:53.940
that you do your job on here

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:55.470
with the international community?

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.240
And I like to flip it around and say

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.660
that the way we do things up here

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.050
actually can inform our politics on Earth.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.600
So, I think we do see this as an example

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.860
of how we can work together
for the greater good,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.920
for greater humanity, and the fact

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:11.810
that we are more alike
than we are different,

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:15.510
and we all have the
same goals at our roots.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.290
- And I think a specific example of that,

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:19.010
both Christina and I launched here

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:21.390
to the International Space
Station having trained

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.575
a majority of our training in Russia,

00:16:23.575 --> 00:16:26.540
at the Gagarin Cosmonaut Training Center

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:27.850
with the Russian Space Agency,

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:30.950
we launched on a Russian
space vehicle to get here,

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:33.760
and for many years now,
that has been our only means

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:34.770
of coming to space.

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:36.590
And so, that is something that,

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.940
especially right now given
the current political climate,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.760
may be surprising to some people,

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:43.280
but for us all up here,
we're working together

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:44.910
as one crew and as one team.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.210
And I think that space has this ability

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:49.750
to actually transcend
some of those problems

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:50.960
that we're having on the ground.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:53.810
So, hopefully we here on the
International Space Station

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:57.180
can provide and maybe serve
as an exemplary role model

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:58.850
for people on the ground as well.

00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:00.650
Jokingly, I've often said maybe a lot

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:02.290
of our world leaders need to come up here

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:04.510
and experience that overview
effect for themselves

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:06.620
to really understand what it means.

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:10.380
- That shouldn't be a joke,
that should be real. (laughing)

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:12.000
time for just a couple more questions,

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:13.500
I just wanna have some fun questions.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.150
So, one of them is, I've
sometimes been criticized

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:18.690
for commenting on the science of movies,

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:22.400
and in the movie "Gravity"
Sandra Bullock's bangs

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:26.780
always pointed down, and
I'm looking at your hair now

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.960
doing it's thing, doing it's zero-G thing,

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:34.710
and so, I'm just curious.

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:35.990
I don't know, I guess
that wasn't a question,

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:40.990
I just wanna tell everyone,
look at your hair in space,

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:44.424
in zero-G, the hair does
not know which way down is.

00:17:44.424 --> 00:17:47.573
(laughing) There you go.

00:17:47.573 --> 00:17:48.406
There you go.

00:17:48.406 --> 00:17:49.239
- Yeah.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:52.710
Yep, that's absolutely true.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:53.900
I think our special effects

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.480
are a little bit better up here, maybe,

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:57.350
than in the movie "Gravity".

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.610
But for me, I was really surprised

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.720
that not only did my
hair not float sideways,

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.350
it actually just goes straight up,

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.360
which makes sense in retrospect,

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.210
but you don't really
even expect that much.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:09.850
- But of course, there
is no sideways in space

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:11.730
if there is not down or up.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.770
So, one final question.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.390
Being in zero-G, presumably,

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.810
would make your stomach
queasy at some point,

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:23.490
certainly initially, so, I am
certain at least one of you

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:27.150
have thrown up in the space
of the Space Station before.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.460
So, how do you clean that up

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:31.880
if it's just scattering everywhere?

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.810
- Actually, both of us have
been extremely fortunate,

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:35.720
and neither of us have had the opportunity

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:37.470
to experience that.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.010
Not that it couldn't still
happen for some other reason,

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:42.260
but I think we're probably
past that risk period.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:43.790
But we do, of course, prepare for it

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.790
because it does strike many astronauts,

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:47.750
and you don't really
know who it's gonna be.

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:49.180
You can't really prepare for it.

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:51.310
So, we have special motion sickness bags

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:52.870
that we fly with us in the spacecraft,

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:55.320
and that we have up here
in case anyone needs them.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.560
Because like you said, of course,

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.650
it's going to be very
difficult to contain.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.610
And I remember seeing that in person

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:04.410
on several flights that I
did on parabolic flights

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:05.930
on what we call the Vomit Comet,

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:08.010
and observing some people that tried

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.670
to utilize the bags and missed,

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:11.900
and that makes for quite a mess,

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.000
especially with a plane

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:15.240
because then you experience the two-G.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.460
But we are equipped,
really, to deal with that

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.790
and to deal with any kind of
medical situations up here

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:21.300
with our training and all of our supplies

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:22.460
on the Space Station.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.800
- Well, excellent, guys.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.360
Thanks for taking the
time for "StarTalk Radio",

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.190
and we got you on video as well.

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:30.320
And so, very much appreciated this.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:31.410
Thank you very much.

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:32.243
- Thank you, Neil.

00:19:32.243 --> 00:19:33.076
It was our pleasure.

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:35.740
- [Mission Control] Station,
this is Houston ACR,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:37.110
that concludes our event

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.330
as we countdown to 20 continuous years

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:40.610
of humans living and working

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:42.616
on the International Space Station.

00:19:42.616 --> 00:19:45.199
(upbeat music)

